1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,362
The Bunker Under Fort Canning Hill

2
00:00:02,362 --> 00:00:11,262
Beneath Fort Canning Hill, a quiet green space in the middle of the city, sits a concrete bunker most visitors to the park above never notice.

3
00:00:11,262 --> 00:00:22,412
Known as the Battlebox, it was once the underground command centre for the British Malaya Command, and it's roughly the size of three basketball courts, buried entirely out of sight.

4
00:00:22,412 --> 00:00:32,725
It was here, in this windowless maze of rooms, that British commanders spent the final days before Singapore fell to Japanese forces in February 1942.

5
00:00:32,725 --> 00:00:44,562
The decision to surrender — one of the most consequential moments in the island's history — was made in this bunker, not in a grand hall or government building, but underground, out of view.

6
00:00:44,562 --> 00:00:48,775
After the war the bunker was sealed up and largely forgotten for decades.

7
00:00:48,775 --> 00:00:59,012
It's since been restored and reopened to the public, filled with reconstructed rooms, wartime documents, and life-sized figures recreating those final tense days.

8
00:00:59,012 --> 00:01:09,750
Walking through it today, lined with old telephones and map tables, gives a strange, quiet sense of just how much history can happen in a space nobody thinks to look for.

9
00:01:09,750 --> 00:01:22,200
Fort Canning Hill's part in the story didn't start or end with the bunker itself — it runs from the hill's pre-war role as the seat of British Malaya Command through to the commanders on both sides of the surrender table.

10
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:36,112
Where it fits in the bigger story: the Battlebox sits alongside other lesser-known wartime sites across Singapore — a reminder that much of the island's most pivotal history happened in unremarkable, easy-to-miss places.

